WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:05.132
When you make a pod in Kubernetes, what&nbsp;
happens behind the scenes?

00:00:05.132 --> 00:00:09.802
How do those Kubernetes components work together&nbsp;to bring that pod into fruition?&nbsp;
&nbsp;

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:13.932
My name is Whitney Lee, I'm on&nbsp;
the cloud team here at IBM.&nbsp;
&nbsp;

00:00:14.582 --> 00:00:16.998
So, this is a cool exercise.

00:00:16.998 --> 00:00:20.000
It kind of&nbsp;goes over all the basics of Kubernetes,

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.584
but with the perspective of a pod being made.

00:00:22.584 --> 00:00:27.060
So, at the basis of our system we have our nodes.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:32.882
The nodes are the worker machines&nbsp;
that that back up the Kubernetes cluster.

00:00:33.311 --> 00:00:36.661
So, in Kubernetes there are two types of&nbsp;
nodes:

00:00:36.661 --> 00:00:40.485
we have the control nodes

00:00:40.485 --> 00:00:45.233
- and in our case we're going to do just one control node&nbsp;
for simplicity's sake,

00:00:45.233 --> 00:00:49.303
but you in a production level cluster you'd want at least 3 control&nbsp;nodes

00:00:49.303 --> 00:00:51.731
- and then we have the compute nodes.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:58.710
So, in our exercise here, we'll do two&nbsp;
compute nodes,

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:03.814
but you could have many many compute nodes in a Kubernetes cluster.

00:01:03.814 --> 00:01:08.689
So, let's talk about a use case where we want to make a pod.

00:01:08.689 --> 00:01:10.646
Let's say I'm the one making&nbsp;a pod.

00:01:10.646 --> 00:01:16.099
So, here's me - my smile, long hair,

00:01:16.367 --> 00:01:18.397
ha ha, my bangs ... okay.

00:01:18.397 --> 00:01:21.523
So, I’m&nbsp;going to make a pod in Kubernetes,

00:01:22.319 --> 00:01:26.560
and when I make that call with a kubectl command.
&nbsp;

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:34.160
That's going to go into the Kubernetes&nbsp;
cluster and hit the Kube-API server.&nbsp;

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:43.760
So, the Kube-API server is the main&nbsp;
management component of a Kubernetes cluster.
&nbsp;

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:53.920
Now, the first thing that the Kube-API server&nbsp;
is going to do with my request to make a pod,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:57.918
is it is going to authenticate it and&nbsp;
validate it.

00:01:57.918 --> 00:02:02.160
So, it's going to say, “Who are you? Do you have&nbsp;
access to this cluster?”&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:08.160
Like, “Oh, you're Whitney! Cool, we&nbsp;
know you. Come on in, make a pod!”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:14.000
So, the next thing that happens is the Kube&nbsp;
API server is going to write that pod to etcd.
&nbsp;

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:21.840
etcd is a key value data store&nbsp;
that's distributed across the cluster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:25.200
and it is the source of truth&nbsp;
for the Kubernetes cluster.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:30.000
(Word on the street is we have a&nbsp;
really good IBM Cloud video about it)&nbsp;
&nbsp;

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:40.160
So, Kube API server writes that request to etcd,&nbsp;
and then etcd will return when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:42.013
it has made a successful write,

00:02:42.013 --> 00:02:49.638
and then, at that point, the Kube-API server already is going to return to me that it's created

00:02:50.312 --> 00:02:56.114
- even&nbsp;though not a lot has happened in our system yet.

00:02:56.559 --> 00:03:00.091
That's because, at its core, Kubernetes

00:03:00.091 --> 00:03:04.459
and etcd has&nbsp;defined a desired state of what the system looks&nbsp;like,

00:03:04.459 --> 00:03:07.591
and then all the Kubernetes components&nbsp;
that we're going to talk about today

00:03:07.591 --> 00:03:11.626
work together to make that desired state equal&nbsp;
to the actual state.

00:03:11.626 --> 00:03:14.219
So, now that we have the pod recorded in the desired state, 

00:03:14.219 --> 00:03:18.457
it is as good&nbsp;as created, as far as Kubernetes is concerned.

00:03:18.724 --> 00:03:22.191
The next component I want to&nbsp;
talk about is the scheduler.

00:03:26.217 --> 00:03:35.460
The scheduler is keeping an eye out for&nbsp;
workloads that need to be created,

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:38.953
and what it's going to do is determine which&nbsp;
node it goes on.

00:03:38.953 --> 00:03:47.979
But what it's doing in the short term is, it's pinging our Kube-API&nbsp;server at regular intervals

00:03:48.314 --> 00:03:52.182
to get a status of whether there are any workloads that need to&nbsp;be scheduled.

00:03:52.182 --> 00:03:53.753
So, usually like five seconds.

00:03:53.753 --> 00:03:59.306
So, the Kube scheduler is ... the scheduler,&nbsp;
excuse me, it's going to ping the Kube API server,

00:03:59.306 --> 00:04:03.651
“Hey, do we have any workloads&nbsp;
that need to be created? No? OK.”

00:04:04.585 --> 00:04:09.451
“How about now? Are there any workloads now?&nbsp;
No? All right.”

00:04:10.502 --> 00:04:11.840
“How about now?”&nbsp;

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:15.900
“Oh, Whitney has a pod that needs&nbsp;
to get created. Let's do that."
&nbsp;

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:22.400
So, now that the scheduler knows that pod needs&nbsp;
to get created on one of our compute nodes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:27.223
let's take a pause from this and talk&nbsp;
about our compute nodes for a moment.&nbsp;

00:04:27.223 --> 00:04:33.824
Our compute nodes have three major&nbsp;
components: one is a Kubelet.
&nbsp;

00:04:35.825 --> 00:04:42.880
The Kubelet is how the compute node&nbsp;
communicates with the control plane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:46.037
or with specifically the Kube-API server.

00:04:46.037 --> 00:04:49.912
So, each each compute node has a Kubelet.

00:04:49.912 --> 00:04:54.549
So, the Kubelet is going to register the node with&nbsp;
the cluster.

00:04:54.549 --> 00:05:00.473
It will send periodic health check so that the Kube-API server knows that our compute&nbsp;nodes are healthy,

00:05:00.473 --> 00:05:06.558
and it will also create and destroy workloads as directed by the Kube-API&nbsp;server.

00:05:06.558 --> 00:05:10.285
Each of our compute nodes is also going to have

00:05:10.285 --> 00:05:16.076
a container runtime engine that's&nbsp;
compliant with container runtime initiative,

00:05:16.438 --> 00:05:22.857
and so, in the past, it's been Docker but it could&nbsp;
really be anything that's compliant -

00:05:23.492 --> 00:05:30.555
and then, finally, it has a Kube proxy which&nbsp;
isn't needed to create our pod today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:34.044
but I would be remiss if I didn't mention it

00:05:34.044 --> 00:05:39.250
- and&nbsp;the Kube proxy is going to help the compute nodes communicate with one another

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:43.186
if there are any&nbsp;workloads that span across more than one node.

00:05:43.948 --> 00:05:46.157
Just generally it helps them communicate.
&nbsp;

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:53.600
Okay, that said, now we have our scheduler.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:57.790
A scheduler is aware that we&nbsp;
need to schedule Whitney's pod.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:02.954
What our scheduler is going to do is look at the&nbsp;
available compute nodes.

00:06:02.954 --> 00:06:08.197
It's going to rule out any that are unsatisfactory either because of&nbsp;limitations

00:06:08.197 --> 00:06:10.444
that maybe the cluster administrator set up,

00:06:10.444 --> 00:06:13.153
or maybe it just doesn't have enough space&nbsp;for my pod,

00:06:13.153 --> 00:06:17.636
and then of the ones that are left it'll choose the best one to run the workload&nbsp;on,

00:06:17.636 --> 00:06:19.840
taking all the factors into account.

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:24.080
Once it has made that choice,&nbsp;
does it schedule the workload?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:28.880
No, all it does is tell the Kube-API&nbsp;
server where it should go.&nbsp;
&nbsp;

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:34.160
Once the Kube-API server knows where it&nbsp;
should go, does it schedule the workload?&nbsp;

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:37.654
No, what it does is it writes it to etcd,

00:06:37.654 --> 00:06:44.720
and&nbsp;then after the successful writes, then we have the desired state versus the actual states and&nbsp;
the Kube-API server knows what it needs to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:49.372
to make that desired state ... the actual state&nbsp;
meet the desired state

00:06:49.372 --> 00:06:56.640
and what that is, is that's when the Kube-API server is going to let&nbsp;the Kubelet know let's say the scheduler said we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:59.144
should run the pod on node 2.

00:06:59.144 --> 00:07:05.065
So, that's when&nbsp;it's going to let the Kubelet know, “On node 2 we need to spin up a pod on this cluster.”

00:07:05.065 --> 00:07:09.273
The Kubelet is going to work together with the container runtime engine

00:07:09.273 --> 00:07:13.735
and make a pod that&nbsp;has the appropriate container running inside.

00:07:14.183 --> 00:07:18.400
So, we have made a pod on a Kubernetes cluster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:21.520
but there's one more management&nbsp;
piece I want to talk about.
&nbsp;

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:30.080
Let's consider a case where, when I made the&nbsp;
pod, I set the restart policy to “always”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:35.331
and then let's say my pod - something happens and it&nbsp;goes down.

00:07:35.331 --> 00:07:40.966
How will the system know that I want a new pod to be created in its place?

00:07:40.966 --> 00:07:44.467
That is where&nbsp;the controller manager comes in.

00:07:44.467 --> 00:07:48.565
This is the last important component of Kubernetes.

00:07:48.565 --> 00:07:52.093
So, the controller manager ...

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:57.190
... it is made up of all of the controllers.

00:07:57.190 --> 00:08:01.869
So,&nbsp;there are many controllers that are controlled by the controller manager.

00:08:01.869 --> 00:08:08.250
And, in particular,&nbsp;the one that's going to help me make a new pod.

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:12.140
For me. I'm not doing anything at&nbsp;
this point, my job is done up there,

00:08:12.140 --> 00:08:18.068
but the controller manager, it's the replication&nbsp;
controller within the controller manager

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:19.912
that's going to help with this task.

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:25.419
So, the controller manager - all the different&nbsp;
controllers are watching different pieces of the&nbsp;&nbsp; Kubernetes system:

00:08:25.419 --> 00:08:29.548
the replication controller,&nbsp;just like the scheduler -

00:08:29.548 --> 00:08:33.793
these controllers are pinging the Kube-API server at a regular basis

00:08:33.793 --> 00:08:37.009
to&nbsp;get an update on the actual state of the cluster

00:08:37.009 --> 00:08:41.268
to make sure the desired state and the&nbsp;
actual states are the same as one another.

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:47.200
So, the replication controller sees,&nbsp;
from contacting the Kube-API server,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:49.337
sees that my pod is gone,

00:08:49.337 --> 00:08:53.840
and it will take&nbsp;the necessary steps to spin that pod back up,

00:08:54.480 --> 00:09:00.000
- or create a new pod, honestly,&nbsp;
because pods are ephemeral.
&nbsp;

00:09:01.294 --> 00:09:09.160
So, in conclusion, all these components&nbsp;
are working together just to make my pod -&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:14.805
and especially we have the Kube-API server, the&nbsp;
main management component of the cluster,

00:09:14.805 --> 00:09:19.040
we have etcd, our data store and our source of truth for&nbsp;the cluster,

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:23.878
the scheduler that helps determine which of the compute nodes the workload should go&nbsp;
on to,

00:09:23.878 --> 00:09:30.742
and the controller manager that is watching the desired state - the actual state - and&nbsp;making sure it's the same as the desired state.

00:09:31.691 --> 00:09:36.316
Thank you. If you&nbsp;have any questions&nbsp;
please drop us a line below.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:39.468
